<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jilin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jilin province</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jilin is a province of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changchun">Changchun</a>. Its lands were part of the ancient kingdoms of Buyeo, Goguryeo and Balhae, and later of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>, which long restricted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> settlement there. From 1932 to 1945 it formed part of the Japanese puppet state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jilin.jpg" alt="Jilin" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dcpeets · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Changbaishan_Tianchi_from_western_rim.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In ancient times the Jilin area was inhabited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a>, Sushen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Mohe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> and Wùjí, and the kingdoms of Buyeo and Goguryeo ruled parts of it. The region then fell successively under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, the Xianbei state, Goguryeo, Balhae, the Khitan Liao dynasty, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchen-jin-dynasty">Jurchen Jin dynasty</a> and the Mongol Yuan dynasty. Under the Qing, much of the area was controlled by the General of Jilin, whose territory extended to much of what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s Primorsky Krai.</p><p>After Primorsky Krai was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1860, the government began to open the area to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> migrants, most of them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, and by the early 20th century the Han had become the dominant ethnic group of the region. In 1932 the area was incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a>, a puppet state set up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changchun">Changchun</a>, then called Xinjing, as its capital. After Japan's defeat in 1945 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> captured Jilin in Operation August Storm, and the region, with the rest of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, passed to the Communists. Manchuria was the staging ground from which they conquered the rest of China in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-civil-war">Chinese Civil War</a>.</p><p>In 1949 the province was smaller, taking in only the environs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changchun">Changchun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jilin-city">Jilin City</a>, with its capital at Jilin City, while Changchun was an independent municipality. It was expanded to its present borders in the 1950s. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> it was enlarged again to include part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>, giving it a border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, but this was later reversed. The name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> phrase girin ula, meaning along the river, written in Chinese as jilin wula and shortened to Jilin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Jilin is one of the three provinces of Northeast China, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to the east and south and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heilongjiang">Heilongjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoning">Liaoning</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>. Its capital and largest city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changchun">Changchun</a>, 113 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jilin-city">Jilin City</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-paektu">Changbai Mountains</a> in the south-east hold its highest peak, Paektu Mountain at 2,744 metres, and the Yalu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumen">Tumen</a> rivers form parts of the border with North Korea. Agriculture centres on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a>, and the province is known for ginseng and deer antlers used in Chinese medicine.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jilin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 698 he defeated a pursuing Tang army under Li Kaigu at Tianmenling and founded a state named Jin near Mount Dongmo, close to modern Dunhua in <b>Jilin province</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/balhae">Balhae · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Xu <b>Jilin</b>, a Shanghai intellectual who reflects liberal voices, agreed that the movement was the root of the Communist Revolution but valued the outcome differently.</blockquote><a href="/movement/new-culture-movement">New Culture Movement · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
